    "body": "\r\n## Motivation\r\nOn Ascend (ENABLE_ASCEND_TRANSFER_WITH_MOONCAKE=1), SGLang fails at startup with:\r\n\r\nRuntimeError: Mooncake Transfer Engine initialization failed.\r\n\r\n\r\nThe shared MooncakeTransferEngine.initialize() wraps engine.initialize(...) in run_with_deadline, which executes the callable on a freshly spawned daemon thread.\r\n\r\nDevice selection is thread-local: torch.npu.set_device() does not propagate from the calling thread to a new thread (pytorch/pytorch#56588). On NPU the device context the Ascend direct transport needs is established on the sglang side — [bootstrap.py](vscode-webview://145o48qm8kk44qos03rfouorc575u7eda3b5c5i5h4fp9l8d9tcp/python/sglang/srt/distributed/bootstrap.py:127) runs torch.get_device_module(device).set_device(get_device().gpu_id) on the main thread, and the Ascend transport relies on it. Since that context is set on the main thread while engine.initialize() runs on the worker thread, the Ascend transport initializes without a device context and returns non-zero.\r\n\r\nOn GPU this never surfaced: Mooncake's C++ layer brings up the NIC context_list_ itself, independently of the torch/ACL current-device state.\r\n\r\n## Modifications\r\n[MooncakeTransferEngine.initialize()] now initializes the engine on the current thread for the Ascend transport, instead of routing it through run_with_deadline:\r\n\r\nAscend (protocol == \"ascend\"): call self.engine.initialize(...) directly on the calling thread — the same thread bootstrap.py selected the device on, and the same thread the later register_memory / transfer_sync calls run on. No thread hop, so the NPU device context is inherently correct and no worker-thread set_device workaround is needed. This also mirrors the memfabric-based [AscendTransferEngine.initialize()], which initializes on the main thread without run_with_deadline.\r\nAll other transports (rdma / efa / tcp): unchanged — still wrapped in run_with_deadline(...) with SGLANG_DISAGGREGATION_ENGINE_INIT_TIMEOUT, so GPU keeps its init fail-fast guard.\r\nThe suffix used for the Mooncake ascend handshake keeps using the physical NPU id (ASCEND_NPU_PHY_ID override), which is distinct from the logical torch id used elsewhere — now documented with a comment.",
